Sogen: a guard page on the thread's own stack grows it as Windows does

Windows grows a thread's stack with a guard region below the committed part
(a page plus the thread's guarantee, which SetThreadStackGuarantee sets).
Touching it does not reach the guest: the kernel clears the faulting page's
guard, moves the guard region down below it and retries the access. When no
room is left above the stack's last page it commits the rest instead, so a
handler has the guaranteed bytes to run in, and raises STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW.
Only a guard page anywhere else is a STATUS_GUARD_PAGE_VIOLATION.

Sogen commits every stack in full, but kernelbase's SetThreadStackGuarantee
still lays its guard region, at the stack's bottom, and Sogen raised
STATUS_GUARD_PAGE_VIOLATION for any guard page. SBCL asks for a 64 KB
guarantee on every thread and turns STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW into a Lisp
STORAGE-CONDITION; a guard page violation it does not handle, so a thread
that ran its stack down took the process with it, exit status 0x80000001.

A guard-page fault inside the faulting thread's stack (TEB DeallocationStack
to StackBase) is now the kernel's stack growth: the guard region moves down
and TEB StackLimit follows it, or the stack is spent and the guest gets
STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW. Elsewhere the fault clears the guard of the faulting
page alone, as Windows does (it used to clear a span measured from the
faulting page but laid from the allocation's base), and is logged with the
thread it hit, since a guest seldom survives one.

